  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.creativeDirection":
    "Over six seconds, reveal an orthographic globe with simplified but recognizable continental outlines. Rotate through the supplied longitude interval, revealing each X only while its surface location faces the viewer; leader lines connect it to a fixed outside label slot. Ease the rotation to a stop, hold the visible markers and their scenario labels, then fade all scene elements to the opaque ground using the shared entrance and exit envelope. Keep the fictional-scenario caption visible with the globe, and never animate explosions, spreading red territory, or a conflict disappearing.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.default.incidents.0.label":
    "Fictional situation A",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.default.incidents.1.label":
    "Fictional situation B",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.default.incidents.2.label":
    "Fictional situation C",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.default.markerMeaning":
    "X = fictional conflict",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.default.scenarioCaption":
    "Fictional scenario on real geography",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.description":
    "A slowly turning globe reveals fictional conflict X markers on recognizable world land outlines. Separate callouts stay distinct so a global overview does not collapse different situations into one story.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.distinction":
    "Unlike orbital-system, the central object is recognizable geographic land with front/back occlusion and surface-anchored incident markers. Unlike network-graph, no edges imply relationships among the incidents.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.domains.0": "Geographic orientation",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.goodFor.0":
    "Open an explicitly fictional global humanitarian scenario",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.goodFor.1":
    "Explain that response planning concerns several separate situations",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.goodFor.2":
    "Introduce a geographical overview before a local logistics example",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.intent":
    "Show the geographic separation of several hypothetical response contexts without suggesting that one action resolves them.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.motionBeats.0":
    "Reveal the supplied setting and identities.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.motionBeats.1":
    "Over six seconds, reveal an orthographic globe with simplified but recognizable continental outlines. Rotate through the supplied longitude interval, revealing each X only while its surface location faces the viewer; leader lines connect it to a fixed outside label slot. Ease the rotation to a stop, hold the visible markers and their scenario labels, then fade all scene elements to the opaque ground using the shared entrance and exit envelope. Keep the fictional-scenario caption visible with the globe, and never animate explosions, spreading red territory, or a conflict disappearing.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.motionBeats.2":
    "Hold the supplied final records and separate derived quantities.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.motionBeats.3":
    "Return to the opaque ground through the shared envelope.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.name": "A world of separate crises",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.notFor.0":
    "The land outline is real geographic context; every conflict location and label is invented and must be marked as such.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.notFor.1":
    "X markers denote separate authored situations, not incident totals, severity, casualty counts, territorial control, or live intelligence.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.notFor.2":
    "No country borders or ownership claims are drawn. Marker visibility follows the chosen view and is not a statement that hidden regions lack need.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.notFor.3":
    "Six seconds supports orientation only; this is not a navigable map or an incident monitoring system.",
  "motionAtlas.fictional-conflict-globe.prompt":
    "Use A world of separate crises (fictional-conflict-globe) for a fictional or approved authored example. Show the geographic separation of several hypothetical response contexts without suggesting that one action resolves them.",
